From f0606dc68aa2e9e91c6feaf63c6ff762d807f885 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eric Parsonage <eric@eparsonage.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 11:03:43 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Keep state shared with analysis workers safe when a
 worker is killed

An analysis worker runs under a custodian that is shut down on timeout,
cancellation or supersession. The worker recorded each module it loaded
in a mutable equal?-based hash table, and the controller read the same
table to find attempts that loaded a changed buffer. A thread terminated
inside hash-set! on such a table can leave every later operation on it
blocked, so the controller's next lookup could hang the server.

The record is now a plain mutable box holding an immutable table, which
the Analysis-Context contract checks. The worker replaces the table with
box-cas! and the controller reads it with unbox, so a worker killed at
any point leaves the box holding a complete table and no lock held.

installed-library? called the setup/dirs finders in the worker. Some of
these calls can force delay/sync promises; killing a worker during an
unfinished initial computation can leave later forces by other threads
blocked indefinitely. The four setup roots are now found once, when the
module is instantiated; current-library-collection-paths is still read
at each call.

A test shuts a worker down inside the production update of the record,
then opens another buffer, which makes the controller read that record,
and requires the open to return. Another test checks that a collection
path added after startup keeps ordinary loading.
